Iron-Rich Foods for Period Days

Iron is an important mineral that helps your body make haemoglobin, which carries oxygen through the blood. Because periods involve blood loss, including iron-containing foods in your diet can be a useful part of maintaining good nutrition.

There are many foods that can contribute iron. Meat, poultry, and seafood provide iron, while vegetarian options include lentils, beans, chickpeas, tofu, spinach, peas, sesame seeds, pumpkin seeds, and fortified foods.

Dal is one of the easiest ways to include plant-based iron in an everyday Indian meal. You can enjoy dal with rice or chapati and add a vegetable side dish. Chickpeas can be used in curries, salads, wraps, or roasted snacks.

Leafy green vegetables are another useful food group. Spinach and other greens can be added to dal, soups, curries, omelettes, or stir-fries. They also provide other nutrients and fibre.

One useful tip is to combine plant-based iron sources with foods containing vitamin C. Tomatoes, lemons, oranges, guava, and bell peppers are examples. For instance, chickpea salad with tomato and lemon or dal with a tomato-based vegetable dish can be simple combinations.

Breakfast can contain iron-rich ingredients too. Oats, fortified cereals, eggs, seeds, or peanut butter can be incorporated into breakfast depending on your dietary preferences.

Tea and coffee are popular drinks, but they contain compounds that can reduce iron absorption when consumed with meals. If you are particularly concerned about iron intake, you may want to avoid drinking tea or coffee immediately alongside your main iron-rich meal.

Food is an important part of general nutrition, but it cannot diagnose or treat iron deficiency. If you experience unusual tiredness, weakness, dizziness, shortness of breath, or very heavy periods, speak with a healthcare professional. They can determine whether you need testing or treatment.

Your diet does not need to become restrictive during your period. Aim for variety and regular meals. Include vegetables, fruits, whole grains, protein sources, and healthy fats throughout the week.

